t the wording is confusing here. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal problem: \"Lilah's family gallery has 400 photos. On a two-day trip... they took half as many... on the first day and 120 more... on the second day. If they added all these photos to the family gallery... calculate the total number of photos in the gallery.\"\n\nSo, the family gallery already has 400 photos. Then during the trip, they took some photos. Then after adding, total is 400 + [first day photos + second day photos]. Therefore, the problem is to find that total. But according to the problem, the problem says that \"they added all these photos to the family gallery\". So the initial 400 is already there, so we need to find the total after adding the new photos.\n\nBut which of the two interpretations is correct? Let me look again:\n\n\"On a two-day trip... they took half as many photos they have in the family's gallery on the first day and 120 more photos than they took on the first day on the second day.\"\n\nSo, perhaps the problem is that on each day, they took half of the total photos they had on that day. Wait, not sure. Maybe the problem is from an original source, might have been clearer. But according to standard math problems, usually, \"half as many as X\" means half of X. But here, \"photographic that took half as many photos they have in the family's gallery on the first day\". The phrase \"half as many photos they have in the family's gallery\" likely refers to half of the family's gallery, which would mean 200 photos on the first day, since the gallery has 400.\n\nBut then they said \"120 more photos than they took on the first day on the second day\". So if first day took 200, second day took 200 + 120 = 320. Then total added would be 200 + 320 = 520, so total in gallery is 400 + 520 = 920.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that \"half as many photos they have in the family's gallery\" is referring to half of the photos they took on that day? Wait, that would be strange becau